# CODEOWNERS — human ownership of the merge gates.
#
# Merges are mechanical: a PR merges when the required checks go green, with no
# human necessarily reading the code. That makes the paths below load-bearing —
# the pipeline must not be able to edit the things that check it. GitHub requires
# a review from the owner below before any change to these paths can merge, even
# under auto-merge. Keep this in sync with the required status checks in branch
# protection.
#
# For this to bind, the human must (once) enable branch protection on the default
# branch with "Require review from Code Owners" turned on. See the README.

# CI (hard gate), the review check + its prompt, the CODEOWNERS file itself, and
# the runner scripts all live under .github/ — one rule covers them.
/.github/                    @GrimsVerk

# Pre-commit (git-level gate).
/.pre-commit-config.yaml     @GrimsVerk

# Any root-level CODEOWNERS, in case one is added later.
/CODEOWNERS                  @GrimsVerk

# The gates' INPUTS, which are load-bearing for exactly the same reason as the
# gates themselves. The review check judges each diff against these documents;
# left unowned, a pull request could rewrite the rule it violates, restate the
# design it diverges from, or adjust the estimates it overran, and the gate would
# grade it against the edited version. The scripts read them at the base commit
# so a single PR cannot do this — CODEOWNERS is what stops it being done in two.
/AGENTS.md                   @GrimsVerk
/CLAUDE.md                   @GrimsVerk
/docs/DESIGN.md              @GrimsVerk
/docs/plans/                 @GrimsVerk

# The oracle's tiebreaker: what the owner values and what they would trade away.
# It is what an unattended agent reaches for when the evidence is ambiguous, and
# the decisions it produces each name the statement here they leaned on — so the
# owner steers by editing this file. An agent that could edit it would be
# steering itself.
#
# Deliberately NOT owned, and this is the pairing that makes the arrangement
# work: /docs/DESIGN.oracle.md, /docs/oracle/ and /docs/plans/oracle/. Ownership
# on those would stop overnight work, which is the whole point of having them.
# They are constrained by .github/scripts/oracle-decisions.sh instead — a
# required check needs nobody awake, where a code owner does.
/docs/VISION.md              @GrimsVerk

# ...and the carve-out that makes it real. `/docs/plans/` above would otherwise
# own the steward's plans too, since a directory rule covers everything beneath
# it. A pattern with NO owner clears ownership for the paths it matches, and
# later rules win — so this line, and only this line, releases the oracle's
# planning directory. Everything else under /docs/plans/ stays owned.
#
# This un-owned path carries TWO kinds of plan: the steward's (citing an
# OD-<n>) and the milestone plans the unattended delivery driver lands (citing
# none, but covering only requirement ids that already exist in a design
# document). oracle-decisions.sh enforces both shapes, which is what replaces
# the owner's review here: a plan on this path can implement landed work and
# can never propose any. Attended plans stay under /docs/plans/ proper, where
# merging past this file IS the owner's ruling.
/docs/plans/oracle/

# The append-only ledgers. Their value is that entries are never quietly
# revised — an escape edited out of the log is an escape that buys no check.
/docs/escapes.md             @GrimsVerk
/docs/acceptance.md          @GrimsVerk

# The Claude permission deny list. Vendor-specific and deletable, but while it
# exists an agent must not be able to widen its own permissions.
/.claude/settings.json       @GrimsVerk

# The rest of the delivery machinery, added after a review found that this file
# and the review prompt both enumerated gate paths, both stopped at .github/,
# and both had been written before the driver existed. What lives here is not
# convenience: deliver-loop.sh holds ORCH_TOOLS — the tool grant every
# unattended orchestrate session runs under — spawn-worker.sh holds the
# per-role grants, and the command files are read at dispatch time and BECOME
# the prompts of unattended agents. A three-line `chore/` branch editing any of
# them is under the planning exemption's size cap and matched no rule here, so
# it merged green with nobody asked to look. That is the same shape as ESC-22:
# a path added after the list that names paths.
/.claude/scripts/            @GrimsVerk
/.claude/commands/           @GrimsVerk
/.claude/agents/             @GrimsVerk
/.claude/orchestration.md    @GrimsVerk

# docs/BACKLOG.md is deliberately NOT listed, and the reason is worth writing
# down because it looks like an omission. It is the left-hand side of the
# authority chain's first arrow — oracle-decisions.sh resolves a decision's
# evidence against BL-<n> ids found here, so a token in this file authorises an
# amendment to the design layer. Owning it would be the obvious fix and it is
# the wrong one: the unattended planner files uncertainties here mid-run, so an
# owner review on this path stops work at 3am, which is the single thing this
# whole arrangement exists to prevent. The honest countermeasure is not approval
# but PROVENANCE and IMMUTABILITY — .github/scripts/backlog-append-only.sh keeps
# landed items from being quietly revised, and every item records the pull
# request that filed it, so the owner reading a decision can see whether its
# evidence came from reality or from the pipeline that wanted it.

# The template-owned glossary. Replaced wholesale by `copier update`, so an edit
# here is not just unreviewed, it is about to be silently lost — words belong in
# GLOSSARY.project.md, which is deliberately NOT owned so it can grow freely
# during a project.
/GLOSSARY.md                 @GrimsVerk
